[AIMessage → tool] create_analysis({'text': 'Sources disagree about the 
authenticity of the Ring of Nestor. Early and mid‑20th‑century Aegean 
archaeologists generally treated the gold signet as an authentic Late Bronze Age
object from Boeotia/Dombrena, using it in reconstructions of Minoan and 
Mycenaean cult iconography. Later scholarship has raised systematic doubts, 
emphasizing its poorly documented provenance, the absence of secure excavation 
records, and stylistic and iconographic features that appear to blend Minoan, 
Mycenaean, Egyptianizing and even modern academic motifs. A balanced treatment 
should (1) present the ring’s traditional dating, cultural attribution and 
iconographic importance; (2) describe the provenance problems and key arguments 
for doubting its authenticity; and (3) explain that many specialists treat it as
a ‘dubitandum’ or possible forgery and therefore avoid relying on it as primary 
evidence for Bronze Age religion. The article should summarize these positions 
with due weight and frame any conclusions about religious or cultural 
significance conditionally on the object’s uncertain status.', 'query_id': 
'20260628181408670265_2c15bdae'})
