provenance and collection history as part of broader debates on authenticity in 
Aegean glyptic.", "Describes the ring’s iconography (multiple human figures, 
ships, trees, altars, possible baetyl, fantastical beings) and notes that this 
unusually complex narrative scene is compared systematically with motifs on 
securely provenanced Cretan and Mycenaean gold rings and frescoes.", "Provides 
sources that situate the ring within broader Aegean and Mycenaean–Minoan 
interactions: it is frequently used—conditional on assumed authenticity—as 
evidence for Minoan–Mycenaean religious imagery, ship cult scenes, sacred trees,
shared symbolism, and interregional cultural contacts.", "Documents 
methodological cautions: recent studies emphasize that, without secure find 
context, the ring should not be treated as key or central evidence for 
reconstructing Minoan–Mycenaean interaction, cult rituals, or religious 
