  "documents": [
    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Angelika Hudler’s German-language study situates the 
Ring of Nestor within the category of early Aegean “Gemmae dubitandae”, i.e. 
engraved gems and rings of doubtful authenticity. The paper explains that the 
ring is widely known under the nickname “Ring of Nestor” but that this 
designation does not imply any genuine ancient ownership. Hudler analyses 
stylistic and iconographic features of the ring in comparison with securely 
provenanced Aegean seals and gold rings, highlighting stylistic peculiarities 
and unconventional image combinations as warning signs for possible forgery. The
text discusses potential prototypes (“Vorbilder”) that modern forgers might have
drawn upon when producing alleged early Aegean gold rings, and places the Ring 
of Nestor among works whose ancient origin can be questioned. Overall, the ring 
is treated as a key case of a potentially modern imitation within the broader 
discourse on problematic Aegean glyptic.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "Hudler’s 2019 English-language paper examines “Gemmae
dubitandae” and their role in exploring Aegean Bronze Age archaeology, using the
Ring of Nestor as a prominent example. She states that debate about the ring’s 
authenticity has been based primarily on observations of stylistic oddities, and
reviews earlier scholars such as Agni Xenaki-Sakellariou who argued against its 
ancient origin. The ring is explicitly classified among gems to be treated with 
suspicion. Hudler stresses that objects lacking secure excavation context and 
traceable collection history, notably including the Ring of Nestor, should not 
serve as central evidence when reconstructing Bronze Age religious spaces and 
rituals. Instead, she proposes that such problematic objects be used to study 
forgery discourse and scholarly practice, rather than as primary data for Aegean
religious iconography.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "This recent English-language paper focuses on the 
iconography of the Ring of Nestor. It emphasizes that the ring’s complex 
narrative scene surpasses the usual compositional norms observed on securely 
provenanced Aegean signet rings, and that this exceptional complexity supports 
suspicions of forgery. The author describes the imagery in detail: multiple 
human figures, ships, trees, altars, a possible baetyl, and 
difficult-to-classify fantastical beings composing what is interpreted as a 
cultic scene by the sea. These motifs are systematically compared to scenes of 
sacred trees and ship-related cult imagery on securely excavated Cretan and 
Mycenaean gold rings and frescoes, noting both thematic parallels and the lack 
of direct precedents for the Ring of Nestor’s dense narrative combination. The 
conclusion underlines that regardless of its ultimate authenticity, the ring has
significantly influenced discussions of Minoan–Mycenaean religious iconography 
and must be explicitly marked as a problematic object when used as comparative 
material.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "This English-language study is devoted to the Ring of
Nestor and reviews arguments for and against its authenticity. It notes that 
past debates focused largely on whether the ring’s iconography accords with 
Bronze Age Aegean styles. Arguments challenging its ancient origin rest mainly 
on the peculiar overall composition and unusual juxtaposition of motifs compared
to securely provenanced rings and seals. The author surveys differences in form 
and technique, but also points out that several iconographic details do have 
parallels in genuine Minoan–Mycenaean works. The conclusion stops short of 
definitively rejecting the ring’s authenticity: it is retained as a dubitandum 
due to its doubtful collection history and lack of excavation record and is 
explicitly described as something that should remain an object of uncertainty 
rather than a cornerstone for reconstructing Aegean religion and cross-cultural 
contacts.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d5",
      "document_summary": "The “Fakes and Dubitanda” chapter in a Cambridge 
volume on Aegean Bronze Age art and archaeology discusses the Ring of Nestor 
within a broader survey of problematic and forged artifacts. The authors state 
that, although in this treatment the ring is considered authentic, doubts about 
it have persisted and are unlikely ever to disappear. The ring is included among
fakes and dubitanda and recommended to be clearly marked as having contested 
authenticity in both teaching and research. The chapter observes that the ring’s
imagery is frequently cited when analysing Aegean religion and cross-cultural 
interaction but argues that it must be handled with particular caution given the
circumstances of its acquisition and the absence of secure provenience. Even 
when provisionally accepted as genuine, the ring is presented as a limited 
reference point that must be used alongside more securely documented material 
rather than as decisive evidence.",
