    {
      "local_ref": "d6",
      "document_summary": "Andreas G. Vlachopoulos’s 2020 chapter “The Ring of 
Nestor and the quest for authenticity” reassesses the authenticity of the Ring 
of Nestor through a comprehensive analysis of its dimensions, materials, 
craftsmanship, iconography, and documentation. He notes that the ring measures 
3.29 cm by 2.18 cm and is the fifth largest Aegean signet ring known. Drawing on
recent research, Vlachopoulos argues that technical and material evidence 
support its authenticity as a Late Bronze Age Aegean artifact and contradict 
earlier claims that it is a modern forgery. The study revisits how early 
archaeologists, notably Arthur Evans, used the ring to construct visual 
narratives of Knossos and “Minoan religion” and engages critically with Hudler’s
categorisation of the ring among gemmae dubitandae. While favouring 
authenticity, Vlachopoulos nevertheless insists that, lacking secure find 
context, the ring must be explicitly labelled as problematic and should be 
employed cautiously, in conjunction with better-provenanced material, when 
discussing Minoan–Mycenaean religion and cultural interaction.",
      "source_ref": "s6"
    }
