{"query": {"id": "20260628181408824383_016fdeef", "handle": "q3", "text": 
"\"Ring of Nestor\" archaeological context", "importance": "supporting"}, 
"items": [{"id": "20260628181526125111_0efae6c3", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"The paper places the Ring of Nestor in a category of Aegean ‘fakes and 
dubitanda,’ examining its supposed findspot and collection history to decide 
whether it reflects real Late Bronze Age religious or social practices.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628181457717413_6c2f1b02"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628181526229622_e8b3c513", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The ring’s scene 
is described as including several human figures, ships, trees, altars, and a 
possible baetyl, which are compared to motifs known from Cretan and Mycenaean 
gold seals and frescoes.", "source_ids": ["20260628181457717413_6c2f1b02"], 
"citations": [3]}, {"id": "20260628181526340295_20f5b41c", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The author argues that if the Ring of Nestor is a modern forgery, its 
iconography reflects 20th‑century scholarly constructs of ‘Minoan religion’ 
rather than historical Late Bronze Age imagery.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181457717413_6c2f1b02"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628181526453017_85226597", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The study 
concludes that without reliable archaeological excavation data, the Ring of 
Nestor cannot be safely used as key evidence for Minoan–Mycenaean interaction or
cult rituals.", "source_ids": ["20260628181457717413_6c2f1b02"], "citations": 
[3]}, {"id": "20260628181526569966_91a4215d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
article recounts that objections to the ancient authenticity of the Ring of 
Nestor have focused on iconography, noting the scene’s unusual complexity and 
unprecedented combination of motifs compared to known Minoan seal traditions.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628181522993840_c61bf0ad"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628181526665238_55b33f24", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The ring’s 
goldsmith work—metal type, manufacturing techniques, and wear marks—is 
systematically compared to that of securely provenanced Minoan and Mycenaean 
rings to assess whether its production fits a Late Bronze Age context.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628181522993840_c61bf0ad"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628181526771953_032f05b3", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The article 
explains that the designation ‘Ring of Nestor’ is linked to associations with 
the Homeric king of Pylos or Palos and its modern collection history.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628181522993840_c61bf0ad"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628181526869494_fd432d24", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Researchers have 
used the Ring of Nestor to support arguments about ship cult scenes, sacred 
trees, and shared Minoan–Mycenaean religious imagery, but the article stresses 
that such use depends on accepting the ring as an authentic ancient artifact.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628181522993840_c61bf0ad"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628181526965683_e02e3507", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Vlachopoulos notes
that the Ring of Nestor carries 14 human figures together with fantastical 
beings, making it one of the Aegean gold seals with the highest number of 
figures and an unusually complex narrative composition.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181457758461_a3e2046b"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628181527063861_fc29b4bb", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The chapter 
compares the ring’s representations of ships, altars, trees, baetyl‑like stones,
and interacting male and female figures to similar themes on frescoes and seals 
from Knossos, Akrotiri, and other Aegean sites to evaluate its adherence to 
known iconographic conventions.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181457758461_a3e2046b"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628181527165636_644e0c97", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The chapter uses 
the Ring of Nestor as a case study within a ‘quest for authenticity,’ exploring 
how unprovenanced artifacts are incorporated into or excluded from scholarly 
narratives about Minoan religion.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181457758461_a3e2046b"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628181527264804_91d1da8c", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Vlachopoulos 
traces positions on the ring’s authenticity from the early 20th century onward 
and argues that, regardless of its authenticity, the ring has shaped scholarly 
understandings of Minoan–Mycenaean cultural contacts, ritual spaces, and 
ship‑related cult imagery.", "source_ids": ["20260628181457758461_a3e2046b"], 
"citations": [4]}, {"id": "20260628181527362569_683a2fb9", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Hudler categorizes the Ring of Nestor among ‘gemmae dubitandae,’ 
pointing out that its archaeological attributes—such as secure findspot and 
excavation documentation—are unclear or lacking.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181523074717_36175ba1"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260628181527456861_26b6497a", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The paper warns 
that using objects without reliable find contexts, including the Ring of Nestor,
as evidence for reconstructing Aegean religious spaces or cross‑cultural 
interactions involves significant methodological risk.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181523074717_36175ba1"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260628181527550050_e4dea55e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Hudler notes that 
accounts of the Ring of Nestor’s discovery rely mainly on secondary descriptions
and collection history rather than contemporary excavation reports, undermining 
the reliability of its archaeological context.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181523074717_36175ba1"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260628181527660007_1541760a", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The paper 
discusses how the ring’s presence in digital publications, online image 
databases, and academic platforms has contributed to its ‘authorisation’ as an 
important artifact for Aegean Bronze Age religion and cultural exchange despite 
the persistent doubts about its authenticity.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181523074717_36175ba1"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260628181527769293_0acc1a00", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Cambridge 
chapter lists the Ring of Nestor as a typical dubitandum and describes its scene
as featuring a woman on a ship, an altar, a sacred tree, and a baetyl, motifs 
that also occur on certain gold rings considered genuine, such as the Minoan 
Ring.", "source_ids": ["20260628181523115159_93e2b977"], "citations": [9]}, 
{"id": "20260628181527875055_3aa10079", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The author 
states that, if genuine, the Ring of Nestor would be a valuable source for 
interpreting maritime rituals, tree cults, baetyl worship, and palace religious 
spaces in the Late Bronze Age Aegean.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181523115159_93e2b977"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628181527979882_a86ebd75", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The chapter 
emphasizes that assessment of the Ring of Nestor’s inclusion in Aegean religious
iconography must consider its archaeological context, material properties, and 
iconographic comparisons, noting that the lack of excavation records leaves its 
status uncertain.", "source_ids": ["20260628181523115159_93e2b977"], 
"citations": [9]}, {"id": "20260628181528077026_bc057fa5", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The chapter compares the Ring of Nestor with other suspected Aegean 
forgeries to explore how archaeologists distinguish authentic Minoan–Mycenaean 
artifacts from later imitations and how these distinctions affect 
interpretations of cross‑cultural interaction and religious symbolism.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628181523115159_93e2b977"], "citations": [9]}], "offset": 
0, "limit": 20, "total": 36, "next_offset": 20}
