{"query": {"id": "20260628182000715230_16e03a44", "handle": "q3.1", "text": 
"Resolve conflict: Across multiple sources the Ring of Nestor is treated both as
potentially authentic Late Bronze Age Aegean glyptic and as a possible modern 
forgery or dubitandum, leading to conflicting implications about its evidentiary
value for Minoan–Mycenaean religion and cultural interaction.", "importance": 
"core"}, "items": [{"id": "20260628182209815002_c19f38b5", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Hudler’s German study describes the Ring of Nestor as a well-known case
within the group of “Frühägäische Gemmae dubitandae” and emphasises stylistic 
peculiarities (“stilistische Eigentümlichkeiten”) and unconventional 
combinations of motifs compared to securely provenanced Aegean seals and rings, 
treating these traits as indicators that its ancient origin can be questioned.",
"source_ids": ["20260628182207442757_3442ada2"], "citations": [14]}, {"id": 
"20260628182209907271_6f91a6f7", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Hudler notes that 
the ring’s nickname “Ring of Nestor” is an “unverwechselbarer Spitzname” and 
clarifies that it does not indicate a real ownership relationship (“solange 
damit kein tatsächliches Besitzverhältnis”), dissociating the modern label from 
any historical attribution.", "source_ids": ["20260628182207442757_3442ada2"], 
"citations": [14]}, {"id": "20260628182209988672_9db168c9", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "(E)scaping arguments states that the debate on the authenticity of the 
so-called Ring of Nestor is “primarily based on the observation of stylistic 
oddities”, and includes it among gemmae dubitandae in which scholars such as 
Agni Xenaki-Sakellariou have expressed doubts about ancient origin.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628181523074717_36175ba1"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260628182210066326_4e58c3d8", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Hudler argues that
artifacts without reliable find contexts and traceable collection histories 
“should not serve as core evidence” for reconstructing Bronze Age religious 
spaces or rituals and explicitly cites the Ring of Nestor as such a problematic 
case.", "source_ids": ["20260628181523074717_36175ba1"], "citations": [8]}, 
{"id": "20260628182210140487_8a12dfcc", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Hudler 
suggests that gemmae dubitandae including the Ring of Nestor are better suited 
for examining forgery discourse and academic practice than for reconstructing 
Aegean religious imagery, thereby limiting their evidentiary use.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628181523074717_36175ba1"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260628182210218377_806507db", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The iconography 
paper characterises the Ring of Nestor’s scene as “far exceeds the compositional
norms” found on securely provenanced Aegean rings and lists its motifs as 
including multiple human figures, ships, trees, altars, a possible baetyl, and 
fantastical beings combined into what is interpreted as a cultic seaside 
ritual.", "source_ids": ["20260628181457717413_6c2f1b02"], "citations": [3]}, 
{"id": "20260628182210302184_a364d14f", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The same 
paper systematically compares scenes on the Ring of Nestor with sacred tree and 
ship cult scenes on securely provenanced Cretan and Mycenaean rings and 
frescoes, concluding that while some motifs are familiar, the ring’s dense 
narrative combination has no direct precedent in authenticated Aegean glyptic.",
"source_ids": ["20260628181457717413_6c2f1b02"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628182210378398_20f36874", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In its conclusion,
the iconography study stresses that “no matter its ultimate authenticity, the 
‘Ring of Nestor’ has deeply influenced discussions on Minoan–Mycenaean religious
iconography” and that modern research must mark it as a “problematic status” 
object whenever it is used as comparative material.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181457717413_6c2f1b02"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628182210452844_ceeb274e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The ResearchGate 
paper remarks that “In the past the authenticity of the so-called Ring of Nestor
has been debated mostly in terms of iconography,” and that “Arguments against 
its ancient origin rest mainly on the peculiar composition and the unusual 
juxtaposition of motifs” when compared to securely provenanced rings and 
seals.", "source_ids": ["20260628181522993840_c61bf0ad"], "citations": [7]}, 
{"id": "20260628182210541300_682d3c71", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The same 
study notes that certain iconographic details of the Ring of Nestor can be 
paralleled in authentic Minoan–Mycenaean works, suggesting that its imagery 
draws on genuine Bronze Age motifs despite its unusual overall composition.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628181522993840_c61bf0ad"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628182210621936_821688d9", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In its conclusion,
the ResearchGate paper states that due to its dubious collection history and 
lack of excavation record, the Ring of Nestor “should remain a dubitandum rather
than a cornerstone” for reconstructing Aegean religion and cross-cultural 
contacts.", "source_ids": ["20260628181522993840_c61bf0ad"], "citations": [7]}, 
{"id": "20260628182210705011_477c1ecf", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
Cambridge ‘Fakes and Dubitanda’ chapter states: “Though considered here to be 
authentic, doubts surrounding the ‘Ring of Nestor’ … have not ceased and 
probably never will,” acknowledging persistent scepticism despite the author’s 
inclination to treat it as genuine.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181523115159_93e2b977"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628182210781681_ed64b86e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The chapter 
recommends that the Ring of Nestor be labelled with “contested authenticity” in 
teaching and research and advises that it “should be handled with particular 
caution, given the circumstances of its acquisition and the lack of secure 
provenience.”", "source_ids": ["20260628181523115159_93e2b977"], "citations": 
[9]}, {"id": "20260628182210855318_d47b636f", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
chapter argues that even if the Ring of Nestor is temporarily accepted as 
authentic, it “should not be treated as decisive evidence” and must be used only
in conjunction with material whose provenance is more secure.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181523115159_93e2b977"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628182210927208_7f7b9d3a", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Vlachopoulos 
provides the dimensions of the Ring of Nestor as 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm and notes 
that it is the fifth largest Aegean signet ring, situating it at the larger end 
of the known corpus of Aegean rings.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181457758461_a3e2046b"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628182211000565_92f563df", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Vlachopoulos 
writes that “Research confirms its authenticity, contrasting earlier claims of 
forgery,” presenting the Ring of Nestor as supported by material and technical 
analysis rather than dismissed as a modern fake.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181457758461_a3e2046b"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628182211074515_de339c6a", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The chapter 
surveys how Evans and other early archaeologists used the Ring of Nestor, among 
other artifacts, to construct visual narratives of Knossos and “Minoan 
religion,” highlighting its historical role in shaping interpretations of Aegean
cult imagery.", "source_ids": ["20260628181457758461_a3e2046b"], "citations": 
[4]}, {"id": "20260628182211155392_be8de144", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"Vlachopoulos explicitly states that, despite his positive assessment of 
authenticity, the Ring of Nestor must be “explicitly labelled as a problematic 
object” and “used with particular caution” alongside securely provenanced 
materials in discussions of Minoan–Mycenaean religion and cultural interaction 
because it lacks a secure excavation context.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181457758461_a3e2046b"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628182236467420_c93e21d1", "type": "Claim", "text": "Modern scholarship 
remains divided on the Ring of Nestor’s authenticity: some authors classify it 
among early Aegean gemmae dubitandae or potential forgeries on stylistic and 
iconographic grounds, while others argue that material and technical analyses 
support its genuineness as a Late Bronze Age Aegean signet ring.", "source_ids":
["20260628181457717413_6c2f1b02", "20260628181457758461_a3e2046b", 
"20260628181522993840_c61bf0ad", "20260628181523074717_36175ba1", 
"20260628181523115159_93e2b977", "20260628182207442757_3442ada2"], "citations": 
[3, 4, 7, 8, 9, 14]}, {"id": "20260628182236982092_405c002f", "type": "Claim", 
"text": "Regardless of whether the Ring of Nestor is judged authentic, recent 
scholarship converges that it should be explicitly labelled as a problematic or 
contested object and used only cautiously, not as core or decisive evidence, 
when reconstructing Minoan–Mycenaean religion or cross-cultural interaction.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628181457717413_6c2f1b02", "20260628181457758461_a3e2046b",
"20260628181522993840_c61bf0ad", "20260628181523074717_36175ba1", 
"20260628181523115159_93e2b977"], "citations": [3, 4, 7, 8, 9]}, {"id": 
"20260628182237441172_a54012cf", "type": "Claim", "text": "The main arguments 
against the Ring of Nestor’s ancient origin concern its peculiar composition and
unusually dense combination of motifs, which far exceed compositional norms 
documented in securely provenanced Minoan–Mycenaean rings and seals.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628181457717413_6c2f1b02", "20260628181522993840_c61bf0ad",
"20260628181523074717_36175ba1", "20260628182207442757_3442ada2"], "citations": 
[3, 7, 8, 14]}, {"id": "20260628182237770705_ac2788df", "type": "Claim", "text":
"Comparative iconographic studies find that individual motifs on the Ring of 
Nestor—such as trees, ships, altars, possible baetyls, human figures, and 
fantastical beings—have parallels in securely provenanced Minoan–Mycenaean 
artworks, even though their dense narrative combination on this ring is 
unprecedented.", "source_ids": ["20260628181457717413_6c2f1b02", 
"20260628181522993840_c61bf0ad"], "citations": [3, 7]}, {"id": 
"20260628182237975930_5f119980", "type": "Claim", "text": "Because the Ring of 
Nestor lacks a secure excavation context and has a dubious or insufficiently 
documented collection history, multiple scholars argue that it should remain 
classified as a dubitandum and not be treated as a cornerstone or core evidence 
for reconstructing Aegean religion or cross-cultural contacts.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181457758461_a3e2046b", "20260628181522993840_c61bf0ad", 
"20260628181523074717_36175ba1"], "citations": [4, 7, 8]}, {"id": 
"20260628182238196788_fe71459d", "type": "Claim", "text": "The nickname “Ring of
Nestor” is a modern label and does not indicate any actual historical ownership 
by the Homeric figure Nestor.", "source_ids": ["20260628182207442757_3442ada2"],
"citations": [14]}, {"id": "20260628182238282352_46f49a5d", "type": "Claim", 
"text": "The Ring of Nestor’s imagery has played a disproportionately 
influential role in the historiography and modern scholarly debates on 
Minoan–Mycenaean religious iconography, shaping early interpretive narratives 
(e.g., those of Evans) and continuing to feature prominently in discussions 
despite its problematic status.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181457717413_6c2f1b02", "20260628181457758461_a3e2046b", 
"20260628181523115159_93e2b977"], "citations": [3, 4, 9]}, {"id": 
"20260628182238516315_42d95ccb", "type": "Claim", "text": "Measurements reported
by Vlachopoulos indicate that the Ring of Nestor is approximately 3.29 cm by 
2.18 cm and ranks among the largest known Aegean signet rings, being described 
as the fifth largest in the corpus considered.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181457758461_a3e2046b"], "citations": [4]}], "offset": 0, "limit": 40,
"total": 26, "next_offset": null}
