submit_final_report({'title': 'Ring of Nestor', 'sections': [{'title': 
'Overview', 'section_type': 'lead', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628181539316891_23313196', '20260628182211296256_0fc39bbb', 
'20260628182211559900_617c31c0'], 'blocks': [{'kind': 'paragraph', 'text': 'The 
Ring of Nestor is a large gold signet ring, traditionally attributed to the 
Aegean Bronze Age and associated with Minoan or Mycenaean cultural spheres, 
whose authenticity and evidentiary value have been the subject of sustained 
scholarly debate.[1][3][4][7][8][9][14] The modern name was coined by Arthur J. 
Evans for a prominent ring within a treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals
reportedly found in a rock-cut tomb near Dombrena, close to Thisbê in Boeotia, 
and he interpreted its elaborate relief scene as a glimpse into a Minoan or 
Mycenaean afterlife.[1][2] Later studies have focused on the ring’s complex 
iconography, unusual composition, and problematic provenance, variously treating
it as an important, if contested, witness to Aegean religious imagery or as a 
probable modern or pseudo-Minoan forgery that should be used only with 
caution.[3][4][5][7][8][9][11][14]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628181633079417_3544d1a6', '20260628181616864683_6a4c8fa1', 
'20260628181617060387_d314f7ac', '20260628181617236604_a7d0170a', 
'20260628181646546771_c88c6105', '20260628182236467420_c93e21d1', 
'20260628182236982092_405c002f', '20260628182238196788_fe71459d', 
'20260628182238196788_fe71459d', '20260628181526125111_0efae6c3', 
'20260628181537597409_1ea31d77', '20260628181538063309_b162314d', 
'20260628181538338881_54b4082c', '20260628181538851687_38bac50f', 
'20260628181539042775_e5d98cc7', '20260628181500042739_cd43216a', 
'20260628181500242049_e33493bb', '20260628181501076211_39c47b46', 
'20260628182209815002_c19f38b5', '20260628182209988672_9db168c9', 
'20260628182210705011_477c1ecf']}]}, {'title': 'Discovery and provenance', 
'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628181539316891_23313196', '20260628182211296256_0fc39bbb', 
'20260628182211559900_617c31c0'], 'blocks': [{'kind': 'paragraph', 'text': 
'Arthur J. Evans introduced the Ring of Nestor in a 1925 monograph describing a 
“sepulchral treasure” of gold signet rings and bead seals from a rock-cut tomb 
near the village of Dombrena, close to Thisbê in Boeotia.[1][2] In his account, 
the treasure derived from a Late Mycenaean burial and included several 
high-status gold rings, among which one particularly large signet ring was 
singled out and given the descriptive label “Ring of Nestor.”[1][2] The 
Heidelberg University Library preserves Evans’s publication in a digitised 
facsimile, which serves as an institutional record for this early presentation 
of the ring and its reported find context.[2][13]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628181454955834_40f8d36f', '20260628181455056219_0185510c', 
'20260628181455147857_8d945568', '20260628181616864683_6a4c8fa1', 
'20260628181617060387_d314f7ac', '20260628181617236604_a7d0170a', 
'20260628182019354901_c7282d04', '20260628182019555974_b82a160a', 
'20260628182019922097_567e4f43', '20260628181859095157_cf42cac0', 
'20260628181859283276_fcd1c529', '20260628181919758455_7a8ad0fd']}, {'kind': 
'paragraph', 'text': 'Subsequent research has questioned the security of the 
ring’s archaeological context and collection history. Hudler notes that accounts
of its discovery rest largely on secondary descriptions rather than contemporary
excavation reports and categorises the Ring of Nestor among early Aegean “gemmae
dubitandae,” highlighting unclear or missing documentation for its findspot and 
acquisition.[8][14] Vlachopoulos similarly reviews the ring’s reported purchase 
and museum history, pointing to gaps and contradictions in the documentation and
concluding that its provenance is insufficiently secure to place it on the same 
footing as excavated objects.[4] These uncertainties underpin broader debates 
over whether the ring should be treated as an authentic Bronze Age artifact or 
as a problematic object of doubtful origin.[3][4][7][8][9][14]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628181537966172_f2d2aeda', '20260628181527362569_683a2fb9', 
'20260628181527550050_e4dea55e', '20260628182209815002_c19f38b5', 
'20260628182209907271_6f91a6f7', '20260628182210066326_4e58c3d8', 
'20260628181538244138_9498c9d4', '20260628181538338881_54b4082c', 
'20260628181538948376_58af6a4e', '20260628181539042775_e5d98cc7', 
'20260628182236467420_c93e21d1', '20260628182236982092_405c002f', 
'20260628182237975930_5f119980']}, {'kind': 'paragraph', 'text': 'The modern 
nickname “Ring of Nestor” is a scholarly label rather than an ancient 
designation and does not imply any historical ownership by the Homeric king 
Nestor.[7][14] Hudler describes the name as an unmistakable modern nickname and 
explicitly notes that it does not indicate a real possession relationship, 
underscoring the distance between the ring’s modern literary association and its
uncertain historical background.[14]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628181526771953_032f05b3', '20260628182209907271_6f91a6f7', 
'20260628182238196788_fe71459d']}]}, {'title': 'Physical description', 
'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628181539316891_23313196', '20260628182211296256_0fc39bbb'], 'blocks': 
[{'kind': 'paragraph', 'text': 'Evans describes the Ring of Nestor as a large 
gold signet ring, significantly larger than an ordinary finger ring, with an 
oval bezel bearing a relief scene.[1] His technical discussion emphasises that 
the ring is made of high-purity gold and was conceived as a signet rather than a
simple personal ornament.[1] The object belongs to a group of “royal” or 
high-status rings within the Thisbê treasure, distinguished by their size and 
craftsmanship.[1]', 'source_ids': ['20260628181455056219_0185510c', 
'20260628181455147857_8d945568', '20260628181616516056_c020c24f', 
'20260628181616712010_8ce66af0', '20260628182019104653_a142dd25']}, {'kind': 
'paragraph', 'text': 'Later measurements provided by Vlachopoulos give the 
dimensions of the ring as approximately 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm, placing it among the
largest known Aegean signet rings and ranking it as the fifth largest example in
the corpus considered.[4] Metallurgical analyses cited in the same study 
indicate a gold content of about 97 per cent, a level consistent with 
high-quality Aegean Bronze Age goldwork, although this material correspondence 
is not regarded as conclusive proof of ancient origin.[4]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628182210927208_7f7b9d3a', '20260628181633636187_53d0d9af', 
'20260628181647432892_64cb0630', '20260628181500463197_a1278665']}]}, {'title': 
'Iconography and imagery', 'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628181539316891_23313196', '20260628182211296256_0fc39bbb'], 'blocks': 
[{'kind': 'paragraph', 'text': 'The bezel of the Ring of Nestor bears a complex 
relief scene that has attracted extensive iconographic analysis. Evans 
interpreted the imagery—featuring trees, divine or supernatural beings, and 
other figures—as a visual representation of the world of the dead and the 
afterlife within a Mycenaean or Minoan religious framework.[1] Subsequent 
descriptions emphasise that the scene includes multiple human figures, ships, 
trees, altars, a possible baetyl stone, and fantastical beings, making it one of
the most densely populated narrative compositions in the corpus of Aegean gold 
seals.[3][4] Vlachopoulos notes that the ring carries fourteen human figures 
alongside non-human entities, an unusually high number for a single signet 
ring.[4]', 'source_ids': ['20260628181455241480_3c2938d2', 
'20260628181616516056_c020c24f', '20260628181526229622_e8b3c513', 
'20260628181526965683_e02e3507', '20260628181633407508_d419ffae', 
'20260628182210218377_806507db', '20260628182226965683_e02e3507']}, {'kind': 
'paragraph', 'text': 'Comparative studies highlight both familiar and atypical 
elements in the composition. Individual motifs such as ships, sacred trees, 
altars, baetyl-like stones, and interacting male and female figures can be 
paralleled in securely provenanced Minoan and Mycenaean seals and frescoes from 
sites such as Knossos and Akrotiri.[3][4][7][9] However, scholars stress that 
the ring’s dense combination of these motifs into a single narrative exceeds the
compositional norms known from authenticated Aegean glyptic, with no exact 
counterpart in excavated material.[3][4][7][9][14] This combination has been 
central to arguments that the scene may reflect a modern synthesis of themes 
rather than a typical Late Bronze Age design.[3][5][6][7][9][14]', 'source_ids':
['20260628181500042739_cd43216a', '20260628181500141983_06aa5879', 
'20260628181527063861_fc29b4bb', '20260628181527165636_644e0c97', 
'20260628181527769293_0acc1a00', '20260628182210302184_a364d14f', 
'20260628182237770705_ac2788df', '20260628181501076211_39c47b46', 
'20260628181501295908_625f5025', '20260628181501503436_796351f2']}, {'kind': 
'paragraph', 'text': 'Several recent studies argue that parts of the scene show 
strong affinities with New Kingdom Egyptian funerary and religious imagery. An 
article on the “pseudo-Minoan” Nestor ring notes that certain postures, 
architectural forms, and tree representations on the ring more closely resemble 
Egyptian compositions than typical Aegean seal images, suggesting that its 
designer may have drawn heavily on Egyptian iconographic models or recombined 
Egyptianised motifs into a new synthesis.[5] On this view, the ring’s imagery is
characterised as an “Egyptianized” imaginative construction of a Minoan world, 
rather than a straightforward reflection of Bronze Age Aegean religious 
practice.[3][5]', 'source_ids': ['20260628181500877313_186162e1', 
