'Evans placed the Ring of Nestor within the Late Mycenaean period, treating it 
as part of a Boeotian tomb group that offered insight into Mycenaean religious 
beliefs and conceptions of the afterlife.[1][2] He interpreted the ring’s 
imagery as evidence for a Minoan-derived religious “after-world” within a 
broader Aegean cultural milieu and compared its craftsmanship to gold rings from
Crete and other Aegean centres.[1] Subsequent scholarship has generally 
maintained a broad Aegean Bronze Age frame for discussing the ring, whether as 
an authentic object or as a later work emulating Minoan–Mycenaean 
styles.[3][4][7][9][14]', 'source_ids': ['20260628181454955834_40f8d36f', 
'20260628181455147857_8d945568', '20260628181455241480_3c2938d2', 
'20260628181616864683_6a4c8fa1', '20260628182019104653_a142dd25', 
