'20260628182236467420_c93e21d1']}, {'kind': 'paragraph', 'text': 'Modern 
discussions differentiate between stylistic attribution and questions of 
authenticity. Comparative iconographic work shows that many of the ring’s motifs
align with themes known from Late Bronze Age Aegean art, even though their 
specific combination is unusual.[3][4][7][9] Metallurgical data indicating high 
gold purity have been cited to argue that the ring’s material properties are 
consistent with Late Bronze Age goldwork, supporting the possibility of an 
authentic Aegean origin.[4][9] At the same time, stylistic peculiarities and the
unprecedented density of the composition have led some scholars to classify the 
ring among early Aegean gemmae dubitandae or pseudo-Minoan objects, potentially 
of modern date.[3][5][6][8][14]', 'source_ids': 
