ring as a key image for reconstructing a Minoan-influenced vision of the 
afterlife, linking its scene to Homeric traditions, Minoan religious imagery, 
and funerary ritual.[1] Later scholars employed the ring to support arguments 
about ship cult scenes, sacred trees, baetyl worship, and shared 
Minoan–Mycenaean religious iconography, often treating it as a major piece of 
evidence for maritime ritual and cultic landscapes.[4][7][9] This early reliance
on the ring contributed to its disproportionate influence in the historiography 
of Aegean religious art.[3][4][9]', 'source_ids': 
