that such compositional features raised doubts about an ancient 
origin.[3][6][7][8][14] Hudler’s studies list the ring among early Aegean gemmae
dubitandae, emphasising stylistic oddities and the lack of secure excavation 
records as reasons to treat it as a problematic object whose ancient status is 
uncertain.[8][14] A separate article on the pseudo-Minoan Nestor ring argues 
that the scene’s Egyptianising features and the mixture of stylistic traits 
