'paragraph', 'text': 'Despite the doubts surrounding its authenticity, the Ring 
of Nestor has maintained a prominent place in modern scholarship on Aegean 
religion and iconography. The ring has been used as a reference point in 
discussions of sacred trees, baetyl stones, ship cults, and the arrangement of 
ritual spaces, and its scene is frequently cited in surveys of Aegean seal 
imagery and analyses of religious art.[3][4][6][7][9] Works such as ICON: Art 
and Meaning in Aegean Seal Images integrate the ring into broader comparative 
frameworks, discussing its stylistic anomalies and the implications of its 
contested status for reconstructing artistic traditions.[6]', 'source_ids': 
