authenticity intersect with broader narratives about Minoan and Mycenaean 
religion.[4][8][14] Hudler and others argue that objects like the Ring of Nestor
are particularly valuable for examining the dynamics of forgery discourse and 
the methodological challenges of working with problematic material, even if they
must be treated cautiously as historical evidence.[8][14]', 'source_ids': 
